changed no code or registry.\n\n**What I reproduced:** I wrote a live lease (deadline an hour ahead) into a review record and gave its holder a non-string value: `[]`, `None` or `0`. In each case the store's reader admits the record and the lease check reads it as unheld. The stale-lease rule doesn't retire it either. In `keep_for` an unheld lease leads straight to taking the lease over and resuming the session. So two calls can end up on one transcript, which is exactly what the lease exists to prevent. With a well-formed string holder, the same call correctly waits and then runs unretained.\n\n**Why not DISMISS:**\n- **Not refuted.** In dispute 008 I said nested lease values need no change, and that was wrong for the holder field. The consumers check that the lease is a dict and that the deadline is a number, but not the holder's type: a falsy non-string becomes \"no holder\". The verdict records the correction.\n- **Not out of scope.** The coordinator's threat-model argument is the one I rejected for DA in 008. Both cases need a mistyped value in the kit's own runtime store, and this lane made \"malformed record means no session\" a supported behaviour. Ruling one in scope and the other out would split the same boundary with no basis in PROCESS.md §6.\n- **Worth fixing.** The fix is small, and the harm is worse than DA's: a silently shared transcript rather than a crash.\n\n**Remedy:** in the reader's one record declaration, require a lease holder that is a non-empty string and a deadline that is a number. Also type the items of `judged`, so no consumed field, top-level or nested, is left undeclared and this fourth round of the same problem is the last. Add a service-level regression showing a mistyped holder gets a fresh session, not a resume. If that changes LLR-270's or TC-351's text, they need another sitting.\n\nThe `.requested` binding file is still untracked, left for the coordinator.","ttft_ms":9425,"type":"result","duration_ms":85003,"uuid":"0b65e78d-f97